# Renaming a volume and formatting one are reached from the drives windows, and each tells its refusal
# in the act's own words before a command exists (README: *Renaming a volume*, *Formatting a volume*).
# The volume driven here is a stand-in whose file system and format offer the scenario names: it holds no
# drive letter, so the act answers for it without any volume of this machine being touched and without a
# consent dialog anywhere.
fake-volume Photos = $T/photos : ntfs : NTFS,exFAT,FAT32
action win.drives
wait-drive Photos
# The Label row is an entry you type into, and the Rename button beside it answers only while the typed
# name differs from what the volume says.
drive-properties Photos
expect-properties-button Rename = none
properties-set label-entry = Family Pictures
expect-properties-button Rename = on
properties-set label-entry = Photos
expect-properties-button Rename = none
# A name longer than the file system holds is cut at a word boundary, and the variant the volume itself
# carries is shown beside the entry before anything is written. A name nothing can carry is refused where
# it is typed: the button appears, because the text differs, and stays shut.
properties-set label-entry = Family Pictures of the summer holidays
wait-properties-text the volume itself carries
expect-properties-button Rename = on
properties-set label-entry = Photos:2026
wait-properties-text cannot hold
expect-properties-button Rename = off
properties-button Close
# The volume this machine runs Windows from is named out of both acts, and its name is no entry at all.
!win drive-properties $C
!win expect-properties-button Rename = none
!win expect-properties-button Format… = off
!win properties-button Close
# The row menu and the properties window open the same format window.
drives-menu Photos : Format…
expect-format-text Everything on it is gone
expect-format-button = off
# The window opens on the file system the volume already holds, and shows only the options that one has a
# switch for: NTFS has a compression row and FAT32 has none, while both have an allocation unit.
expect-format-text Compress new files by default
expect-format-text Allocation unit size
format-set fs-choice = FAT32
expect-no-format-text Compress new files by default
expect-format-text Allocation unit size
format-set fs-choice = NTFS
# The name the volume is called, and the shorter one it carries.
format-set volume-name = Family Pictures of the summer holidays in 2026
expect-format-text the volume itself carries
# The format button answers once the volume is named by hand, and the words it asks for are shown beside
# the entry that types them.
expect-format-text Type /dev/fake-Photos to format
expect-format-button = off
format-set confirm-entry = /dev/fake-Photos
expect-format-button = on
# Quick is ticked from the start, and the tool states that `/Q` overrides the pass count, so the pass count
# is no row at all while quick is ticked: it comes back when the tick goes away and goes again when it
# returns. A row the choice does not carry answers nothing, so the act is never handed both at once.
expect-no-format-text Overwrite passes
format-set quick = off
expect-format-text Overwrite passes
format-set quick = on
expect-no-format-text Overwrite passes
# Pressing it hands the choice to the act, which answers for a stand-in naming no drive: on Windows there
# is no letter to format, and over there the options of the table belong to this system's tool. Neither
# answer starts a tool of any kind, and the window closes either way.
format-accept
# The answer is the act's own words, told in a window: on Windows a stand-in naming no drive letter cannot
# be formatted at all, and over there the switches of the table are this system's format tool's own words.
!win wait-dialog This volume has no drive letter to format
!unix wait-dialog the options of the format table belong to this system
!win dialog-button This volume has no drive letter to format : Close
!unix dialog-button the options of the format table belong to this system : Close
expect-format-button = none
# The properties window of the same volume answers again afterwards.
drive-properties Photos
expect-properties-button Format… = on
